# The working tree must reproduce the committed bytes, exactly.
#
# Why this file exists
# --------------------
# `scripts/check_surfaced.py` transitively imports `experimentL/harness/
# execute_recipe.py` -> `experimentJ/harness/macro_v2.py`, whose import-time
# `verify_v1_source()` hashes `experimentI/harness/gate_I.py` byte-exactly
# against a hash frozen in `experimentJ/expected.json`. Every file in that chain
# is frozen evidence or a historical harness, so none may be edited -- which
# leaves the bytes on disk as the only free variable. `core.autocrlf` is `true`
# at system level on at least one development machine, so a fresh clone gets a
# CRLF working tree and that guard reports VOID on an artifact that never
# changed.
#
# Why `-text` and NOT `text=auto eol=lf`
# --------------------------------------
# `-text` disables end-of-line conversion in BOTH directions: on checkout the
# working tree receives the blob verbatim, and on `git add` nothing is
# normalized. That is exactly the invariant the frozen guards need, and no more.
#
# `* text=auto eol=lf` would also fix the checkout, but it would additionally
# make Git normalize on the way IN. 171 tracked files -- every one of them under
# `work_interface/`, the frozen W1 experiment packs -- have CRLF in their
# committed blob. Under `text=auto` a later `git add` touching any of them would
# silently rewrite frozen experiment evidence to LF. That is the exact class of
# defect this repository exists to prevent, so the broader attribute is the
# wrong tool here even though it is the more common idiom.
#
# Nothing in this file changes committed content. It changes what a checkout
# puts on disk.
#
# Existing checkouts
# ------------------
# Git applies attributes when it WRITES a file. It does not rewrite files
# already present in a working tree, so a clone made before this file existed
# keeps its CRLF copies and the frozen guards keep reporting VOID. For those:
#
#     python scripts/normalize_worktree_eol.py            # report
#     python scripts/normalize_worktree_eol.py --apply    # rewrite EOL-only files
#
# See issue #7 and docs/development/initiatives.md I-7.

* -text
